    "question": {
      ":question/id": "f7b999d0-4074-59fb-8dda-ad2ab31206e0",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following lines is parallel to $-12x+3y=0$?",
      ":question/worked-solution": "For two distinct nonvertical lines to be parallel, they must have the same slope.\n\nTo find the slope of $-12x+3y=0$, convert to slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-12x+3y &= 0 \\\\\n3y &= 12x \\\\\ny &= 4x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe slope is the coefficient of $x$, so the required slope is $4$.\n\nOnly $y=4x+2$ has slope $4$.\n\nThe line $y=4x+2$ has slope $4$ and $y$-intercept $2$, different from $0$. Therefore, it is a distinct parallel line.\n\n$$\ny=4x+2\n$$",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
